+- **Memory Complexity:** Matrix-free implementation reduces memory overhead by storing only gradients and moments, not the Hessian or normal equations [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Stable for ill-conditioned systems [S1, unofficial]. Slower than computing $A^T A$ and $A^T b$ for moderately sized problems [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Handles ill-conditioned problems well; supports $L_1$ and $L_2$ norms; matrix-free (memory efficient); stable with Adam filtering [S1, unofficial][S3].
+- **Limitations:** Per-parameter filtering can cause high-frequency artifacts under noise, anisotropy, or early termination [S3]. Assumes no parameter correlations [S3].
+- **When to use:** Large-scale or rank-deficient problems where memory is constrained and stability is prioritized over speed [S1, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When high-frequency detail preservation is critical and gradients are noisy/anisotropic, or when faster convergence is needed for well-conditioned moderate-sized problems [S1, unofficial][S3].
 
 ## Background
-The Gradient Descent Solver, particularly in its Adam variant, was introduced by Kingma and Ba in 2015 [S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in differentiable volume rendering frameworks such as DiffVox to optimize voxel grid parameters [S2, unofficial]. The method addresses gradient noise and anisotropy by filtering gradients temporally across iterations using exponential moving averages [S3].
